      {
        "id": "def-projected-contact-dynamics-and-euler-step",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Projected contact dynamics and explicit projection Euler step",
        "deps": [
          "def-kkt-residual-and-normal-cone",
          "lem-projection-onto-a-closed-convex-set-has-the-variational-inequality",
          "thm-nearest-point-in-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set",
          "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "def-absolutely-continuous-function",
          "def-derivative",
          "def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere",
          "def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $P_C(y)$ be the unique nearest point in $C$ to $y$, whose existence and uniqueness are [[thm-nearest-point-in-a-nonempty-closed-convex-set]]. For $x\\in C$, set\n$$N_C(x)=\\{n\\in\\mathbb R^d:\\langle n,z-x\\rangle\\le0\\text{ for every }z\\in C\\};$$\nfor $x\\notin C$, set $N_C(x)=\\varnothing$. On $C$ this is the outward normal cone of [[def-kkt-residual-and-normal-cone]]. The projection variational inequality of [[lem-projection-onto-a-closed-convex-set-has-the-variational-inequality]] says $P_C(y)=p$ exactly when $p\\in C$ and $y-p\\in N_C(p)$.\n\nFor $T>0$, a solution of the projected contact dynamics with forcing $f:[0,T]\\times C\\to\\mathbb R^d$ and initial state $x_0\\in C$ is an absolutely continuous map $x:[0,T]\\to C$ satisfying\n$$f(t,x(t))-\\dot x(t)\\in N_C(x(t))\\quad\\text{for almost every }t\\in[0,T].$$\nEquivalently, $\\dot x(t)+N_C(x(t))\\ni f(t,x(t))$. For an integer $N\\ge1$, put $h=T/N$, $t_k=kh$, and define the exact explicit projection Euler nodes by\n$$x_{k+1}=P_C\\bigl(x_k+h f(t_k,x_k)\\bigr),\\qquad k=0,\\ldots,N-1,$$\nstarting from $x_0$. Its affine interpolant joins consecutive nodes linearly on each interval $[t_k,t_{k+1}]$.\n\nFor a map $x:[0,T]\\to\\mathbb R^d$, **absolutely continuous** means that each coordinate is absolutely continuous in the scalar sense of [[def-absolutely-continuous-function]], and $\\dot x(t)$ is the vector of coordinate derivatives, from [[def-derivative]], wherever they all exist. The solution condition requires a Lebesgue-null set $N\\subseteq[0,T]$ such that for every $t\\notin N$ all coordinate derivatives exist and the displayed inclusion holds. Here **almost every $t$** is relative to Lebesgue measure $\\lambda$ from [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]], with the convention of [[def-measure-null-set-and-almost-everywhere]].\n"
      },
