      {
        "id": "thm-projected-euler-square-root-global-error",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "A square-root global error bound for projected Euler with certified projection tolerances",
        "deps": [
          "thm-projected-euler-existence-uniqueness-and-convergence",
          "def-projected-contact-dynamics-and-euler-step",
          "alg-certified-projected-euler-method",
          "lem-normal-cone-maximal-monotone-and-projection-resolvent",
          "lem-projection-onto-closed-convex-set-is-nonexpansive",
          "lem-projected-euler-projection-residual-certificate",
          "thm-gronwall-integral-inequality",
          "thm-c1-lipschitz-ac-bv-hierarchy",
          "thm-bv-functions-are-differentiable-almost-everywhere",
          "lem-the-product-of-two-absolutely-continuous-functions-is-absolutely-continuous",
          "thm-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus-for-absolutely-continuous-functions",
          "thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm",
          "lem-exponential-dominates-one-plus-x",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-dependent-choice",
          "thm-heine-borel-rn"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "statement": "Assume the hypotheses of [[thm-projected-euler-existence-uniqueness-and-convergence]], including Countable Choice and Dependent Choice, and let $x$ be its unique solution. Let $D$ be any finite bound for the diameter of $C$, set\n$$M=\\|f(0,x_0)\\|_2+L(T+D),\\qquad C_0=8M^2+L^2(1+M)^2T,\\qquad K_{\\mathrm{err}}=\\sqrt{C_0T e^{(2L+1)T}}.$$\nFor $h=T/N$, let $X_h$ be the affine interpolant of the exact projection Euler recurrence. If a complete run of [[alg-certified-projected-euler-method]] returns nodes $\\widetilde x_k$ with certified residuals\n$$\\sup_{z\\in C}\\langle \\widetilde x_k+h f(t_k,\\widetilde x_k)-\\widetilde x_{k+1},z-\\widetilde x_{k+1}\\rangle\\le\\varepsilon_k^2,$$\nand $\\widetilde X_h$ is their affine interpolant, then\n$$\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|\\widetilde X_h(t)-x(t)\\|_2\\le K_{\\mathrm{err}}\\sqrt h+e^{LT}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\\varepsilon_k.$$\nIn particular, the exact-projection branch has uniform error $O(\\sqrt h)$. Certified projection errors with $\\sum_k\\varepsilon_k=O(\\sqrt h)$ retain that rate; no rate follows from an uncertified solver tolerance alone.\n\n"
      }
